Chapter: RecheckCore

 Verdict vocabulary and type comparison for the independent rechecker.

 Type comparison answers THREE values, not two. A rechecker that cannot
 decide must say so: collapsing the undecided case into equal manufactures
 agreement it did not earn, and collapsing it into unequal manufactures a
 disagreement against a program that is fine. Both are worse than silence.
 docs/Designs/Active/Tools/IndependentRechecker.md section 6 is the rule.

Section: Verdicts

  RcVerdict =
   | RcAgree
   | RcDisagree
   | RcUnsupported

  RcFinding = record {
   rf-def : Text,
   rf-verdict : RcVerdict,
   rf-kind : Text,
   rf-detail : Text
  }

  rc-finding : Text, RcVerdict, Text, Text -> RcFinding
  rc-finding (d) (v) (k) (det) =
   RcFinding { rf-def = d, rf-verdict = v, rf-kind = k, rf-detail = det }

  rc-verdict-atom : RcVerdict -> Text
  rc-verdict-atom (v) =
   when v
    is RcAgree -> "AGREE"
    is RcDisagree -> "DISAGREE"
    is RcUnsupported -> "UNSUPPORTED"

Section: Walk State

  RcWalk = record {
   rw-findings : List RcFinding,
   rw-fuel : Integer
  }

  rc-walk-new : Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-walk-new (fuel) = RcWalk { rw-findings = [], rw-fuel = fuel }

  rc-burn : RcWalk -> RcWalk
  rc-burn (w) = RcWalk { rw-findings = w.rw-findings, rw-fuel = w.rw-fuel - 1 }

  rc-dry : RcWalk -> Boolean
  rc-dry (w) = w.rw-fuel <= 0

  rc-add : RcWalk, RcFinding -> RcWalk
  rc-add (w) (f) =
   RcWalk { rw-findings = list-push (w.rw-findings) f, rw-fuel = w.rw-fuel - 1 }

Section: Three Valued Type Comparison

  TyCmp =
   | TyEq
   | TyNeq
   | TyUnknown

 A definite difference in any component is a definite difference overall,
 so TyNeq outranks TyUnknown here: FunTy a r and FunTy b r differ whatever
 is unknown about r once a and b are known to differ.

  rc-cmp-both : TyCmp, TyCmp -> TyCmp
  rc-cmp-both (a) (b) =
   when a
    is TyNeq -> TyNeq
    is TyUnknown -> when b is TyNeq -> TyNeq is otherwise -> TyUnknown
    is TyEq -> b

  rc-bool-cmp : Boolean -> TyCmp
  rc-bool-cmp (b) = if b then TyEq else TyNeq

  rc-ov-eq : OverflowMode, OverflowMode -> Boolean
  rc-ov-eq (a) (b) =
   when a
    is OvError -> when b is OvError -> True is otherwise -> False
    is OvWrapping -> when b is OvWrapping -> True is otherwise -> False
    is OvClamping -> when b is OvClamping -> True is otherwise -> False

  rc-rw-eq : RealWidth, RealWidth -> Boolean
  rc-rw-eq (a) (b) =
   when a
    is RwF64 -> when b is RwF64 -> True is otherwise -> False
    is RwF32 -> when b is RwF32 -> True is otherwise -> False

  rc-rm-eq : RealMode, RealMode -> Boolean
  rc-rm-eq (a) (b) =
   when a
    is RmDefault -> when b is RmDefault -> True is otherwise -> False
    is RmTrapping -> when b is RmTrapping -> True is otherwise -> False
    is RmSaturating -> when b is RmSaturating -> True is otherwise -> False

 The atom `error` on the wire is not only ErrorTy: ir-emit-type has no arm
 for ProofTy, PropEqTy, TypeCon or TypeApply, and its `is otherwise` sends
 all four to the same spelling. Four distinct types and one failure type
 are indistinguishable here, so every one of them is undecided.

  rc-ty-opaque : CodexType -> Boolean
  rc-ty-opaque (t) =
   when t
    is ErrorTy -> True
    is TypeVar (i) -> True
    is ForAllTy (i) (b) -> True
    is ForAllEff (i) (b) -> True
    is ProofTy -> True
    is PropEqTy (a) (b) -> True
    is TypeCon (n) -> True
    is TypeApply (f) (a) -> True
    is otherwise -> False

  rc-ty-eq : CodexType, CodexType -> TyCmp
  rc-ty-eq (a) (b) =
   if rc-ty-opaque a | rc-ty-opaque b then TyUnknown
   else rc-ty-eq-known a b

 A unit type is documented as transparent to its inner type at assignment
 boundaries, and the exact extent of that is not settled by the language
 docs. Answering Unknown rather than guessing is deliberate; do not
 "fix" it to Eq or Neq without a documented rule to cite. Resolving it by
 reading strip-unit-ty is the one move section 7 forbids.

  rc-ty-eq-known : CodexType, CodexType -> TyCmp
  rc-ty-eq-known (a) (b) =
   when a
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (m) ->
     when b
      is IntegerTy (lo2) (hi2) (m2) -> rc-bool-cmp (lo == lo2 & hi == hi2 & rc-ov-eq m m2)
      is UnitTy (n2) (i2) -> TyUnknown
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is RealTy (w) (m) ->
     when b
      is RealTy (w2) (m2) -> rc-bool-cmp (rc-rw-eq w w2 & rc-rm-eq m m2)
      is UnitTy (n2) (i2) -> TyUnknown
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is TextTy -> when b is TextTy -> TyEq is UnitTy (n2) (i2) -> TyUnknown is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is BooleanTy -> when b is BooleanTy -> TyEq is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is CharTy -> when b is CharTy -> TyEq is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is VoidTy -> when b is VoidTy -> TyEq is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is NothingTy -> when b is NothingTy -> TyEq is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is ListTy (e) -> when b is ListTy (e2) -> rc-ty-eq e e2 is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is LinkedListTy (e) -> when b is LinkedListTy (e2) -> rc-ty-eq e e2 is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is FunTy (p) (row) (r) ->
     when b
      is FunTy (p2) (row2) (r2) -> rc-cmp-both (rc-ty-eq p p2) (rc-ty-eq r r2)
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is SumTy (n) (args) (ctors) ->
     when b
      is SumTy (n2) (args2) (ctors2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is ConstructedTy (n2) (args2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is RecordTy (n) (args) (fs) ->
     when b
      is RecordTy (n2) (args2) (fs2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is ConstructedTy (n2) (args2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is ConstructedTy (n) (args) ->
     when b
      is ConstructedTy (n2) (args2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is SumTy (n2) (args2) (ctors2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is RecordTy (n2) (args2) (fs2) -> rc-nominal-eq (n.value) args (n2.value) args2
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is EffectfulTy (e) (s) (r) ->
     when b
      is EffectfulTy (e2) (s2) (r2) -> rc-ty-eq r r2
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) ->
     when b
      is UnitTy (n2) (inner2) -> rc-cmp-both (rc-bool-cmp (n.value == n2.value)) (rc-ty-eq inner inner2)
      is otherwise -> TyUnknown
    is VectorTy (n) (t) ->
     when b
      is VectorTy (n2) (t2) -> rc-cmp-both (rc-bool-cmp (n == n2)) (rc-ty-eq t t2)
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is VectorMaskTy (n) ->
     when b is VectorMaskTy (n2) -> rc-bool-cmp (n == n2) is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is LinearTy (inner) -> rc-ty-eq inner b
    is otherwise -> TyUnknown

Section: Assignability

 Equality is the wrong question in an argument position and using it there
 was this rechecker's first bug, caught by its own control: passing a
 value of `Integer between 0 and 20` to a plain `Integer` parameter is
 ordinary Codex, and equality called it a disagreement.

 A value flows from a narrower band into a wider one, never the reverse.
 The reverse direction is the one that matters and it stays a
 disagreement, which is what the compiler itself does -- an unproven range
 in a bounded slot is CDX2051, refused rather than guarded.

 Overflow mode is not a range, so a difference in mode is not a fit that
 can be decided by containment. Undecided rather than guessed.

  rc-ty-fits : CodexType, CodexType -> TyCmp
  rc-ty-fits (from) (to) =
   if rc-ty-opaque from | rc-ty-opaque to then TyUnknown
   else when from
    is IntegerTy (lo1) (hi1) (m1) ->
     when to
      is IntegerTy (lo2) (hi2) (m2) ->
       if rc-ov-eq m1 m2 == False then TyUnknown
       else rc-bool-cmp (lo2 <= lo1 & hi1 <= hi2)
      is otherwise -> rc-ty-eq-known from to
    is FunTy (p1) (row1) (r1) ->
     when to
      is FunTy (p2) (row2) (r2) -> rc-cmp-both (rc-ty-fits p2 p1) (rc-ty-fits r1 r2)
      is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is ListTy (e1) -> when to is ListTy (e2) -> rc-ty-fits e1 e2 is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is LinkedListTy (e1) -> when to is LinkedListTy (e2) -> rc-ty-fits e1 e2 is otherwise -> TyNeq
    is otherwise -> rc-ty-eq-known from to

 One type has two spellings on this wire and they meet each other. A sum or
 record type is emitted INLINE with its constructors where it stands on its
 own, but a RECURSIVE reference inside its own constructor field list is
 emitted as the truncated `(ctd "Expr" (args))`, deliberately: the emitter
 says re-emitting the fields caused exponential IR bloat on recursive
 types. So the constructor `Mul` carries a parameter typed as the inlined
 `(sum "Expr" ...)` while the same sum's own field list for `Mul` says
 `(ctd "Expr" (args))`. They denote one type and structural equality called
 them different, which reported four disagreements on `expr-calculator` and
 eleven on `final-batch-test` in the first full sweep, all of them this
 rechecker's own.

 Nominal identity is the right comparison for both spellings: same name and
 same type arguments is the same type. A different name still disagrees, so
 this widens nothing except across the spelling boundary.

  rc-nominal-eq : Text, List CodexType, Text, List CodexType -> TyCmp
  rc-nominal-eq (n) (args) (n2) (args2) =
   if n /= n2 then TyNeq
   else if list-length args == 0 | list-length args2 == 0 then TyEq
   else rc-ty-eq-list args args2

  rc-ty-eq-list : List CodexType, List CodexType -> TyCmp
  rc-ty-eq-list (xs) (ys) =
   if list-length xs /= list-length ys then TyNeq
   else rc-ty-eq-list-loop xs ys 0 (list-length xs) TyEq

  rc-ty-eq-list-loop : List CodexType, List CodexType, Integer, Integer, TyCmp -> TyCmp
  rc-ty-eq-list-loop (xs) (ys) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else rc-ty-eq-list-loop xs ys (i + 1) len (rc-cmp-both acc (rc-ty-eq (list-at xs i) (list-at ys i)))

